WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.070
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here today,
and I'm gonna tell you don't aspire

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:09.060
to fluency in the language you're
learning, and I'm gonna tell you why.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:15.630
You'll notice by the way that I removed
my, uh, backdrop with a fake bookshelf.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:19.350
Uh, people were complaining, uh,
that, uh, I never seen to take any of

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:22.950
those books down from the bookshelf,
and it was creased and whatnot.

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:25.260
So I decided to try a different look.

00:00:25.265 --> 00:00:26.075
Tell me what you think.

00:00:26.385 --> 00:00:29.775
Anyway, why do I say
don't aspire to fluency?

00:00:30.375 --> 00:00:35.345
This kinda came to me after reading
Atomic Habits, and I have in the

00:00:35.349 --> 00:00:40.004
past said, you know, we should try to
become fluent if, uh, we're climbing

00:00:40.004 --> 00:00:43.935
a mountain, if our goal isn't to reach
to the peak of the mountain, that we're

00:00:43.935 --> 00:00:47.084
certainly never gonna get there, that
you should have these lofty goals.

00:00:47.084 --> 00:00:53.504
However, reading Atomic Habits made
me realize the important thing is

00:00:53.504 --> 00:00:58.500
not to have some lofty goal but
rather, how are we gonna get there?

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:05.399
So the focus should not be on imagining
yourself speaking fluently, although

00:01:05.405 --> 00:01:09.090
that's, it's not a bad thing to
do, but it's not gonna help you.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:13.110
The, the main focus has to
be on what am I going to do

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:15.810
every day to improve every day?

00:01:15.815 --> 00:01:21.570
Because in fact, working on a language,
as I've talked about in a variety of my

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:25.170
videos, can be a bit of a lonely journey.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.750
We can have the impression that
we're putting a lot of effort

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.640
in and not getting anywhere.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.110
And yeah, maybe the sense that one day
I will be fluent is, um, helpful to us.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:42.930
But what is more helpful is to have
that sense that every day that I'm doing

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:49.125
something, I am improving and even to have
activities that give you small victories.

00:01:49.515 --> 00:01:53.985
So I have enjoyed, for example,
doing these matching pairs and

00:01:54.015 --> 00:01:56.505
assembling sentences on LingQ.

00:01:57.255 --> 00:02:02.745
Uh, you know, it slows me down in terms
of acquiring more vocabulary is time

00:02:02.745 --> 00:02:07.515
that is not spent on massively, you know,
ingesting the language, listening and

00:02:07.515 --> 00:02:09.375
reading, getting my brain used to it.

00:02:09.824 --> 00:02:13.095
But it is diving into the nuts
and bolts of the language.

00:02:13.445 --> 00:02:17.775
It's giving me a small sense of
achievement because those are

00:02:17.775 --> 00:02:19.935
essentially easy tasks to do.

00:02:20.175 --> 00:02:22.395
So we get an easy sense of achievement.

00:02:22.935 --> 00:02:24.465
It keeps me going.

00:02:24.645 --> 00:02:28.425
I'm focused on the now, what
I'm doing now to improve in the

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:32.865
language, what I'm doing now to get
a sense of success in the language.

00:02:33.285 --> 00:02:37.005
Uh, another thing could be seeking
out an opportunity to use the

00:02:37.445 --> 00:02:40.425
language with a native speaker,
maybe who lives in your community.

00:02:40.935 --> 00:02:44.745
uh, maybe getting on a
online lesson with a tutor.

00:02:45.045 --> 00:02:48.915
All those little things that
keep you moving along, uh, it's

00:02:48.915 --> 00:02:50.955
fine to have that lofty goal.

00:02:51.285 --> 00:02:54.945
Uh, a number of the languages
that I say that I speak, in

00:02:54.945 --> 00:02:56.265
fact, I don't speak fluently.

00:02:56.805 --> 00:03:03.000
Uh, I can maybe get them up to fluent
at some point, but that doesn't mean I

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.310
haven't enjoyed trying to learn them.

00:03:05.549 --> 00:03:10.290
I haven't enjoyed every step along the
way, and I will enjoy going back to them.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:15.105
And so the fact that I didn't
achieve fluency, In Greek or

00:03:15.105 --> 00:03:17.895
Romanian or Czech or Korean even.

00:03:18.435 --> 00:03:21.705
Although I'm fairly well along,
I have a lot of vocabulary, a

00:03:21.705 --> 00:03:25.095
high degree of comprehension,
but I can't claim to be fluent.

00:03:25.335 --> 00:03:26.955
Fluent to me is B2.

00:03:27.285 --> 00:03:31.815
The vast majority of language
learners never reach B2.

00:03:32.145 --> 00:03:32.475
Okay?

00:03:32.685 --> 00:03:35.745
So therefore, all those people
haven't been wasting their time.

00:03:36.075 --> 00:03:40.485
All those people, myself included,
for the bulk of my languages, we've

00:03:40.485 --> 00:03:45.105
been doing something very rewarding
uh, enabling us to discover another

00:03:45.105 --> 00:03:49.095
language, to get interested in the
people, in the culture behind the

00:03:49.095 --> 00:03:53.435
language, to have small victories
when we're able to use the language.

00:03:53.625 --> 00:03:56.355
All of those things are
tremendously positive.

00:03:56.565 --> 00:04:02.985
So if the only criterion of success in
language learning is to become fluent,

00:04:03.285 --> 00:04:07.505
most language learners are failures,
and I don't believe they are failiures.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:11.400
I don't consider myself a
failure in those languages that

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.080
I don't speak fluency fluently.

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:17.670
It's been tremendously award rewarding,
discovering those languages and

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:19.019
learning about those countries.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:23.370
And I do things every day that give
me satisfaction in the language.

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:28.800
Even though I am a long way from
fluency, I may never achieve fluency in

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.800
Persian or Arabic or Turkish or Greek.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:36.075
However, whatever I have learned in
those languages is something that I

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:37.865
have and no one can take away from me.

00:04:38.155 --> 00:04:42.775
As Lomb Kato, the famous Hungarian
polyglot once said, A language

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:47.265
is one of those things that is
worth learning, even imperfectly.

00:04:47.535 --> 00:04:51.645
And I think that's a very important
context to have as we study.

00:04:51.795 --> 00:04:54.975
Think of it as something very
valuable, worthwhile doing.

00:04:55.155 --> 00:04:56.844
You can continue to improve.

00:04:57.165 --> 00:04:59.505
You will get as far as
you can in the language.

00:04:59.685 --> 00:05:03.105
The longer you stay with it,
the better you will get, the

00:05:03.105 --> 00:05:07.965
closer you'll get to fluency, but
you may never achieve fluency.

00:05:08.085 --> 00:05:11.535
And still it will have
been a worthwhile journey.

00:05:11.655 --> 00:05:15.675
And that's why I say, you know,
in learning languages, I shouldn't

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:20.145
say don't aspire to fluency, but
don't be fixated with fluency.

00:05:21.135 --> 00:05:21.765
There you have it.

00:05:21.765 --> 00:05:22.695
Thank you for listening.

00:05:22.725 --> 00:05:23.035
Bye.

